      "why": "Unrolled_k with k=2-4 can achieve 1 result/cycle throughput (ceil(N/k)+3 <= 4 cycles for N<=14, k>=4; for k=2, N=10 gives 8 cycles which is too slow, but k=3-4 with N=10-12 gives 4-7 cycles). Actually, for >=1 MSPS at reasonable Fmax, we need results/cycle >= 1, so unrolled_k only qualifies if ceil(N/k)+3 <= Fmax/1000... wait, throughput = Fmax / (ceil(N/k)+3). For 1 MSPS at 100 MHz, need ceil(N/k)+3 <= 100, which is always true. But the spec says >=1 MSPS, and unrolled_k produces 1 result every ceil(N/k)+3 cycles. At 100 MHz, that's 100M/(ceil(N/k)+3) results/sec. For N=12, k=4: ceil(12/4)+3=6 cycles, so ~16.7 MSPS. This meets throughput. Unrolled_k may have lower area than pipelined for small N since it shares the datapath across k rotations. Worth exploring for the area-minimum corner."
